The function uses `head_dim` internally for frequency computation: `channel_range / head_dim`. When we pass `rope_dim=64` instead of `head_dim=128`, the frequency range changes — `inv_freq = 1/base^(k/64)` for k in [0,2,...,62], vs the original `1/base^(k/128)`. This means the rotated dims get a denser frequency spectrum covering the full range, which is actually better — each rotated dimension carries more positional information per dim.

The output cos/sin will have shape `[1, seq_len, n_head, 32]` (rope_dim//2 = 32), and `apply_rotary_emb` will correctly rotate only the first 64 dims and pass through the remaining 64.

Everything checks out. The implementation is clean, minimal, and backward-compatible (ROPE_FRACTION=1.0 recovers original behavior).

